 the effective config both before and after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6563b104dbf740e9\",\"cmd-ea65b8062592403f\",\"cmd-8bc3f8dc3ded4b9f\",\"cmd-f6a97d69cc2c4bcf\"]},{\"id\":\"policy-enforced\",\"requirement\":\"Policy actually rejects new passwords under 12 chars or with fewer than 3 character classes, and accepts compliant ones, via a genuine non-root self-service password change\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A pty-driven passwd session as a real non-root test user showed clear rejections ('password is shorter than 12 characters', 'password contains less than 3 character classes') and an eventual clean acceptance of a 13-char/3-class password with 'password updated successfully'; the old password subsequently failed to authenticate, confirming the hash actually changed. The executor correctly identified and avoided the root/chpasswd bypass path that skips pwquality.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4267554803ea4811\",\"cmd-d1dee0b8ef194001\",\"cmd-3726f9cd30ff4efb\"]},{\"id\":\"policy-persistence-restart\",\"requirement\":\"Policy and enforcement survive a node restart (independent restart-evidence protocol applied to node1)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-reboot boot_id (4d340bf4...) and healthy policy/PAM wiring were captured, a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' was acknowledged, the node became unreachable during the transition and then returned with a different boot_id (91095322...), and post-reboot checks confirmed the pwquality settings and PAM wiring persisted and live enforcement (reject 8-char, accept compliant 13-char/3-class) was re-confirmed with a fresh test user.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8bc3f8dc3ded4b9f\",\"cmd-57aad345f7a04f72\",\"cmd-12642e015fed4de9\",\"cmd-25b99312b02e446d\",\"cmd-d00648072b1f4ec5\",\"cmd-f6a97d69cc2c4bcf\",\"cmd-e111040684cd4a86\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.6,\"summary\":\"Most executor-created state was properly cleaned up: both test users (pwtestuser, pwreboottest) were deleted and confirmed gone, and /tmp/pwtest.py was removed at the end. However, three items were left behind or unexplained: (1) cmd-ea65b8062592403f created a timestamped backup file /etc/security/pwquality.conf.bak.<epoch> that is never removed in any later command; (2) /tmp/reboot.log (redirect target of the backgrounded shutdown command) persists in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot with no cleanup step; (3) the after-executor lifecycle snapshot shows a newly failed systemd unit, 'user@1000.service', not present at before-prepare — most plausibly a side effect of killing/deleting test users (pwtestuser/pwreboottest, likely UID 1000) while their pty-driven passwd sessions and logind user sessions were still active (cmd-6973b1db4f40479a shows a userdel initially blocked by a running process later force-killed in cmd-9733a22d47c04b92). This failed unit was never diagnosed or cleared. Also notable: cmd-ea65b8062592403f's first-pass tests, using 'passwd --stdin' as root, showed the enforcement bypass ('UNEXPECTED ACCEPT') the executor then correctly worked around with the pty-based self-service tests — a legitimate diagnostic step, not a flaw.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ea65b8062592403f\",\"cmd-6973b1db4f40479a\",\"cmd-9733a22d47c04b92\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's material outcome — requiring new user passwords on node1 to be at least 12 characters with at least three character classes — is satisfied with strong, directly observed evidence: policy configuration, genuine non-root self-service enforcement testing (reject-short, reject-low-class, accept-compliant, verified hash change), and full persistence/enforcement re-verification across a real reboot with a changed boot identity. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also satisfied for node1 with a clear pre-restart healthy baseline, acknowledged restart, differing post-restart boot id, and recovered configuration and live behavior. Hygiene is moderate: test users and the temporary test script were cleaned up, but a config backup file, a reboot log, and a newly failed 'user@1000.service' systemd unit were left behind without explanation or remediation.\",\"limitations\":[\"The exact cause of the post-executor 'user@1000.service' failed state is not directly diagnosed in the captured commands; it is inferred from the timing and nature of the pty-based user session testing rather than confirmed.\",\"Full contents of the pty test python scripts are partially obscured by redaction markers in the captured stdout, slightly limiting exact verification of prompt-matching logic, though the printed pass/fail outcomes are unambiguous.\",\"No evidence was captured showing whether the leftover /etc/security/pwquality.conf.bak.* file or /tmp/reboot.log pose any operational risk beyond minor residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"policy-config","requirement":"Configure password policy on node1 requiring minlen=12 and minclass=3, wired into PAM","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline showed pam_pwquality already requisite in system-auth/password-auth. Executor set minlen=12 and minclass=3 in /etc/security/pwquality.conf and a new pwquality.conf.d/99-benchmark-policy.conf, confirmed present in the effective config both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6563b104dbf740e9","cmd-ea65b8062592403f","cmd-8bc3f8dc3ded4b9f","cmd-f6a97d69cc2c4bcf"]},{"id":"policy-enforced","requirement":"Policy actually rejects new passwords under 12 chars or with fewer than 3 character classes, and accepts compliant ones, via a genuine non-root self-service password change","status":"satisfied","summary":"A pty-driven passwd session as a real non-root test user showed clear rejections ('password is shorter than 12 character